metaverse what an interesting word a  word that has been making quite some  noise for a few months now and will  probably impact us for years to come  metaversa as a term was first coined in  the 1992 science fiction novel  snowcrafts by neil stevenson as a  portmanteau of meta and universe meta is  a greek word that means after and is  used in the english language in  different ways it could denote a change  in condition like metamorphosis it could  denote something of a higher or second  order kind like metal language or in  colloquial english meta has become an  adjective which essentially means  extremely self-aware  self-reflective or self-referential as  in the phrase that's so meta in the case  of the metaverse the word is used quite  liberally and it means a virtual  universe a world where people are  connected to a virtual reality and  interact with each other the idea of a  virtual world has always been an  alluring topic for sci-fi writers most  of them consider it a dystopian reality  and neil stevenson goes as far as to  call the people constantly connected to  the metaverse gargoyles due to their  grotesque appearance today the metaverse  attempts to enter the vernacular and  disrupt our realities in a more subtle  way facebook has announced that it will  invest heavily in the metaverse more  specifically it will create software and  hardware that will allow humans to have  a fully immersive virtual or augmented  reality experience also in the crypto  sphere many companies have invested in  the idea of a metaverse by creating  virtual worlds where you can buy virtual  land or compete in virtual games so what  should our reaction be to all this is  the metaverse going to be a virtual  paradise or lay the groundwork for a  future dystopia  in the metaverse everybody has an avatar  a simulacrum of the real you we are  essentially trying to map the real world  onto a virtual reality the basic premise  of the metaverse is that it can offer an  alternative reality where some  limitations of the real world can be  transcended imagine a seems-like world  that is way more advanced and  sophisticated the idea started because  we tend to become more interconnected  than global and we need technologies  that can eliminate whatever limits our  connections in the metaverse we can  interact with people we want to know we  can discover worlds that exist in our  fantasies and compete or cooperate in  ways that are more immersive and  interesting the workplace can be a  virtual or augmented reality inside  one's apartment and the way people  collaborate can be as good as in the  real world sometimes even better in the  crypto world metaverse projects can  create new digital worlds where people  can exchange tokens  buy virtual land create their own  virtual kingdoms and probably come up  with innovative solutions when it comes  to government and coexistence all in all  the metaverse aims to become the  instigator for the creation of new  worlds inside our real world that will  automatically offer new opportunities to  people around the globe a benevolent  plan but is it really that way  i have said before that dystopias exist  everywhere and nowhere they are fed by  our demons and are projected outward as  physical creations that reflect the  color of our inner world by 2030  depression will be the number one cause  of illness worldwide dystopias are  essentially a manifestation of our  depression we can't really escape a  dystopian future for dystopias are  already here in many places around the  world dystopias are created due to lack  of planning corruption and false  prioritization people get carried away  due to social pressure and create  mediocre solutions that gravitate  towards more quantity and not more  quality i guess the virtual world  symbolizes our inability to make the  best out of our current world that's an  idea that i believe i can hear  percolating in the minds of many of my  viewers we need escapes and the  metaverse can be such an escape in the  metaverse the world won't be more real  but it can be what we want it to be  without us working really hard to make  it so it is a very alluring narrative  but with a crucial caveat in the  metaverse the world will be a simulation  with the companies who own this  simulation controlling everything you  see and experience there is some room  for virtual free will so to speak but  the world will be designed in a way that  will focus mainly on maximizing profit  for their respective companies  additionally because the metaverse will  be designed to feel addictive people  will lose touch with reality we are  already absorbed by fantasies  inside our phones and our computers  imagine what can happen if the space  that nurtures these fantasies  becomes even more immersive and isolated  our ability to physically connect will  eventually diminish  the real world will feel more and more  hostile and our mental issues will  become an actual pandemic the thing is  that with most technologies our ability  to partake in the decision making over  their adoption  is out of our hands and i'm not sure how  the companies that want to put forth  such technologies can maintain a certain  degree of responsibility towards their  users american technology ethicist  tristan harris puts it very nicely so  the real problem of humanity  is that we have paleolithic emotions  medieval institutions  and god-like technology  as said by e.o wilson the father of  sociobiology and the problem is that  these three operate on three different  clock rates what happens when your  god-like  is accelerating faster than your  steering wheel which is your medieval  institutions  and your paleolithic emotions your  ape-like instincts are guiding the whole  thing  so when i was a kid i was a magician and  magic is all about studying the limits  of our paleolithic emotions and while we  were all looking out for the moment when  technology would overwhelm human  strength that's when it takes our jobs  that's when it's smarter than us we miss  the much earlier point when technology  actually undermines human weaknesses and  this is the point that i would argue all  the things we're experiencing are going  wrong with technology because when  technology hacked our cognitive limits  our mental limits we feel that as  information overload  when it hacked our dopamine system we  feel that as addictive use when it hacks  our social validation we feel that as  mass narcissism everyone has to be an  instagram celebrity  when it hacks our confirmation bias we  feel that as fake news that we want to  it feels good to get information that  already confirms our beliefs and when it  hacks our outrage we feel that as  polarization and when it hacks our trust  the foundations of what we know to trust  or be true we feel that is deep fakes or  post-truth  and when you look at what this set of  things causes information overload leads  to shortening attention spans social  isolation teen depression suicide  breakdown of sense making conspiracy  theories and a post-truth world and  these are not disconnected problems  because they live in a connected system  of mutually reinforcing harm  and we call this system human  downgrading we have to fix it  i personally think that the timing for  the metaverse is not right i read in a  blog post that with this sort of change  its arc is as long and unpredictable as  its end state is lucrative i understand  that facebook wanted to capitalize on  the fact that people are used to work  and live from home due to our current  predicament but the mechanics of our  mental apparatus are not ready to allow  us to get fully immersed in virtual  reality sure some people might like it  and vr is a fun toy for some but our  brain's natural state has many  limitations it's easy to get addicted to  stimulation generated by technology but  we know that this is just a transient  feeling we already have good  technologies in place that allow us to  interact with each other i don't really  think that metaverse is solving a  problem and that opposes basic  entrepreneurship principles that suggest  that companies need to actually solve  problems unless we decide to create a  problem i can't really predict how the  future will look like i see the world  changing and evolving and i want to stay  optimistic the exposure to information  makes people smarter and more capable  but the complexity of our systems seems  so insurmountable that constantly  creates problems that each generation  needs to face i have said before that we  need to slow down slowing down allows us  to reflect deeply on our contemporary  issues and solve them in a more  effective way at the moment countries  and companies are part of a  technological arms race that keeps  nourishing our obsession with growth and  profits we focus more on feeding our  collective neurosis and we rarely  introspect instead of having a fruitful  philosophical dialogue about the future  of humanity and what constitutes  sustainability and prosperity we  aimlessly produce and consume this  dialogue will inevitably happen at some  point  hopefully it won't be too late  hey guys hope you enjoyed today's video  if you did make sure to like subscribe  turn on notifications  and comment below something cool please  so that more people can discover it  if you want to watch more videos from my  channel check out this one and this one  take care see you soon adrian out  you
 

